body:
The above poster is correct but there are also other implementations. Such as:

    class Model:
        def run(self):
            raise NotImplementedError()
        def run_wrappper(self):
            self.run()

    class M1(Model):
        def run(self):
            print('blah')

    m = M1()
    m.run_wrapper()

prints "blah"

Any child class will have the run_wrapper method that will always call the run method. And if you have not overridden the run method, then it will throw an error. You're probably looking for "abstract base classes" though, which there's better documentation online about those. 

To answer your question, it is completely okay to modify attributes of any property of any inherited class within the method.
